//! repo_gates — run the three checks over ONE named repository and come back
//! with every finding.
//!
//! This is the GENERIC gate workflow. It takes the repository as an input, so
//! it works against any cargo workspace on the box serving the queue — not
//! only this one. Its sibling `gates.awl` is the aion lane battery: seven legs
//! wired to this repository's own shape (the console's bun suite, the embed
//! freshness check, the battery's own worker crate) and returning each leg's
//! exit code beside a tail of its log. Both are real; they answer different
//! questions. Ask `gates` "did the aion battery pass". Ask this "what is wrong
//! in this repository, exactly".
//!
//! WHAT COMES BACK IS THE FINDINGS, NOT A SUMMARY. Every diagnostic arrives as
//! a value with its file, line, column, lint name, and message; every failing
//! test arrives with everything it printed, which for a panicking test is the
//! whole assertion block including the line it panicked at. Both outcomes carry
//! the per-gate exit codes as well, so a reader never has to infer from a
//! finding count whether a gate ran.
//!
//! THE RED OUTCOME CARRIES EVERYTHING THE GREEN ONE DOES, AND MORE. A verdict
//! that drops its evidence on the way to being a verdict makes the reader
//! re-run the gates to learn anything, which is the whole fault this document
//! exists to close.
//!
//! WHY NO `timeout`, ON THE ACTION OR THE RUN. How long a full build, lint and
//! test pass takes is a property of the repository being gated, and this
//! document cannot know it. A ceiling written here would be a number nobody
//! named, and the direction it fails in is the bad one: it would report an
//! honest slow build as a broken one. With no declared timeout the activity
//! waits exactly as long as it always has, and with no declared retry it runs
//! exactly once — a broken measurement surfaces rather than being re-attempted
//! on a schedule nobody chose. An operator who wants a bound names it by
//! cancelling the run.
//!
//! THE TYPES BELOW ARE DUPLICATED IN `repo_gates_worker.awl`, AND THAT IS #225.
//! AWL has no import. The two copies are byte-identical on purpose and must
//! move together; the server refuses a worker whose advertised descriptors do
//! not match the deployed contract, and that refusal is the only thing holding
//! them equal today.
//!
//! THE TWO INPUTS.
//!
//! - `repo` — absolute path to the cargo workspace to gate.
//! - `runner` — absolute path to `repo-gates.zsh` on the box serving the queue.
//!   It is an input rather than a written-out path because this queue gates any
//!   repository while the script lives with aion, so its location is a property
//!   of the box, not of the work.
workflow repo_gates
  input repo: String
  input runner: String
  outcome clean: type Clean, route success
  outcome red:   type Red,   route failure

/// One gate command's own result. `finding_count` is that gate's share of the
/// deduplicated findings, so the three counts and the `findings` list are two
/// views of one measurement rather than two tallies that can disagree.
type GateOutcome { gate: String, exit_code: Int, finding_count: Int }

/// One diagnostic, at the resolution needed to fix it: where it is, which lint
/// or error code raised it, and what it says.
///
/// `line` and `column` are 0 when the compiler attached no source location —
/// linker warnings arrive that way, under `#[warn(linker_messages)]`, with no
/// span at all. Zero cannot collide with a real reading because rustc's lines
/// and columns are 1-based, and `file` reads `unknown` in the same case.
///
/// rustc's child notes are deliberately absent. They are mostly instructions
/// for SILENCING a lint — `#[allow(...)]` and its family — and handing those to
/// a reader whose job is to fix the code is handing them the bypass alongside
/// the defect.
type Finding {
  gate: String,
  level: String,
  file: String,
  line: Int,
  column: Int,
  lint: String,
  message: String,
}

/// One failing test, with everything it printed. For a panicking test that is
/// the whole assertion block including the source file and line it panicked at,
/// so the failure needs no summary written beside it.
type FailedTest { test: String, stdout: String }

/// The whole measurement, from one repository, in one document.
///
/// `raw_finding_count` is the count BEFORE byte-identical duplicates were
/// collapsed. `--all-targets` compiles a crate's library and its test target,
/// and rustc emits the same diagnostic once for each, so one defect arrives
/// twice per gate; the collapse is recorded here rather than performed
/// silently, because two copies of one defect is not a more exact count, it is
/// a count that misrepresents how much is wrong.
///
/// `passed` is computed from what actually ran: every gate exited zero AND no
/// findings came back AND no test failed. All three are required because any
/// one can be true while another is false — `--keep-going` can leave a zero
/// exit beside real diagnostics, and a gate can die non-zero having emitted
/// nothing.
///
/// `instruction` travels with the findings and says to fix them at their cause,
/// never to silence them. It is carried in the report rather than assumed by
/// whoever reads it.
type GateReport {
  repo: String,
  /// "git" or "none" — never absent, so a reader never has to tell "this tree
  /// is not under version control" from "this report predates the field".
  vcs: String,
  /// The commit the gates measured; the empty string exactly when `vcs` is
  /// "none". A gate run against a dirty tree is evidence about that dirty
  /// tree, and a report carrying only a path cannot say which one it was.
  head_commit: String,
  /// `git status --porcelain` verbatim, empty for a clean tree. Carried as the
  /// LINES themselves rather than a count or a hash: a count cannot attribute,
  /// and a hash has no preimage the reader holds.
  porcelain: String,
  gates: [GateOutcome],
  raw_finding_count: Int,
  errors: Int,
  warnings: Int,
  findings: [Finding],
  failed_tests: [FailedTest],
  passed: Bool,
  instruction: String,
}

/// Nothing to report. The per-gate exit codes ride along anyway, so a green
/// verdict can still be checked against what actually ran rather than taken on
/// the word `clean`.
type Clean { repo: String, gates: [GateOutcome] }

/// Everything that is wrong, at full resolution.
type Red {
  repo: String,
  gates: [GateOutcome],
  errors: Int,
  warnings: Int,
  raw_finding_count: Int,
  findings: [Finding],
  failed_tests: [FailedTest],
  instruction: String,
}

worker repo_gates
  /// Served by `repo_gates_worker.awl` — one declared command running
  /// `repo-gates.zsh` on the worker's own host. Bodyless here because a
  /// workflow document declares what it REQUIRES of a queue; the body lives
  /// with the queue that serves it.
  action run_gates(runner: String, repo: String) -> GateReport

step gate
  run_gates(runner: runner, repo: repo) -> report
  outcome green: when report.passed,
    route clean(repo: report.repo, gates: report.gates)
  outcome findings: otherwise,
    route red(repo: report.repo, gates: report.gates, errors: report.errors,
      warnings: report.warnings, raw_finding_count: report.raw_finding_count,
      findings: report.findings, failed_tests: report.failed_tests,
      instruction: report.instruction)
